{%- comment -%}
  Variant picker — one control group per entry in product.options_with_values,
  so it fits Size, Colour, Length or whatever a merchant actually configured.
  Nothing here is hard-coded to a two-option apparel catalogue: "colour" only
  changes the PRESENTATION (photo swatches instead of value tiles), and the
  size-guide link only appears when the option name contains "size".

  MARKUP CONTRACT (theme.js reads every one of these by name on the PDP AND
  re-emits the same classes into the quick-view dialog):
    .opt / .opt__head / .opt__label / .opt__value
    .swatches > .swatch[data-swatch] / .swatch--img
    .sizes > .size-btn
    [data-opt-btn][data-opt-index][data-opt-value] + aria-pressed
    [data-opt-current="<index>"]  — the live value echo
    [data-size-opt] + [data-size-home] — the phone sheet MOVES the size group
      into the bar and puts it back before this anchor, which is why the anchor
      is a separate element and not a marker class on .opt itself.

  UNAVAILABLE VALUES ARE KEPT AND MARKED, NEVER DROPPED. Removing a sold-out
  size hides the fact that the size exists, so a shopper who wears it reads the
  store as not making it at all. They also stay FOCUSABLE — aria-disabled and a
  data attribute, never the disabled attribute — so a keyboard or screen-reader
  shopper can still land on the value and hear that it is gone.

  The two availability attributes and their different lifetimes are explained at
  the point they are computed; the short version for the stylesheet is that
  [data-unavailable] is permanent and [data-unavailable-here] is only true of
  the selection Liquid rendered, so it must be gated behind html:not(.js).

  Params: product · v (current variant) · color_opt_index · size_chart_html
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- unless product.has_only_default_variant -%}
  {%- for option in product.options_with_values -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign opt_index = forloop.index0
      assign opt_down = option.name | downcase

      assign is_color = false
      if opt_down == 'color' or opt_down == 'colour'
        assign is_color = true
      endif
      assign is_size = false
      if opt_down contains 'size'
        assign is_size = true
      endif

      comment
        Option NAME localisation only. The option VALUE is never touched here:
        the alt-text colour parser in pdp-media-colors matches on the value
        exactly as imported, and a translated value would match no photograph.
      endcomment
      assign opt_label = option.name
      if is_color
        assign opt_label = 'product.option_color' | t
      elsif is_size
        assign opt_label = 'product.option_size' | t
      endif
    -%}
    {%- comment -%} A single-value option is not a choice — it is a fact about the
    product, and a one-button picker just asks the shopper to confirm something
    they cannot change. It stays out of the picker and remains in the variant
    id carried by the form. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if option.values.size < 2 -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}

    <div class="opt pdp-opt"{% if is_size %} data-size-opt{% endif %}>
      <div class="opt__head">
        {%- comment -%} Name left, live value beside it, size-guide link hard right on
        the same row. The size group used to print no value at all on the theory
        that the pressed button says it — which is true only while the button row
        is on screen, and it is the first thing the phone sheet moves away. {%- endcomment -%}
        <p class="opt__label lbl-sm mute">
          {{- opt_label | upcase | escape -}}:
          <strong class="opt__value" data-opt-current="{{ opt_index }}">{{ v.options[opt_index] | default: '' | upcase | escape }}</strong>
        </p>
        {%- if is_size and size_chart_html != blank -%}
          <button type="button" class="tlink opt__guide" data-open-size-chart>{{ 'product.size.chart_link' | t }}</button>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      {%- if is_color -%}
        <div class="swatches" role="group" aria-label="{{ opt_label | escape }}">
          {%- for value in option.values -%}
            {%- comment -%} A colour is picked from its own photograph. The curated
            alt-tagged media wins (same contract as the gallery), the variant's
            featured media is only the fallback. {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign cv_media = false -%}
            {%- assign value_down = value | downcase -%}
            {%- assign cv_needle = ' in ' | append: value_down | append: ',' -%}
            {%- assign cv_needle2 = ' in ' | append: value_down | append: '-' -%}
            {%- assign cv_needle3 = ' i ' | append: value_down | append: ',' -%}
            {%- assign cv_needle4 = ' i ' | append: value_down | append: '-' -%}
            {%- for cv_product_media in product.media -%}
              {%- if cv_product_media.media_type == 'image' -%}
                {%- assign cv_product_alt = cv_product_media.alt | default: '' | downcase -%}
                {%- if cv_product_alt contains cv_needle or cv_product_alt contains cv_needle2 or cv_product_alt contains cv_needle3 or cv_product_alt contains cv_needle4 -%}
                  {%- assign cv_media = cv_product_media -%}
                  {%- break -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            {%- unless cv_media -%}
              {%- for cv_variant in product.variants -%}
                {%- if cv_variant.options[opt_index] == value and cv_variant.featured_media != blank -%}
                  {%- assign cv_media = cv_variant.featured_media -%}
                  {%- break -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            {%- endunless -%}
            {%- comment -%} THE MERCHANT'S OWN SWATCH WINS.
            Shopify lets a merchant set a colour or an image per option value in
            admin, and this theme never read either: swatches came from matching
            the colour NAME against a 60-entry English fashion-colour table in
            theme.js. So a store selling "Sage", "Petrol" or anything in French
            configured its swatches, saw nothing change, and had no way to find
            out why. A value the merchant has set is a fact; the table is a
            guess, and a guess must never beat a fact. {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if value.swatch.image -%}
              <button type="button" class="swatch swatch--img" data-opt-btn data-opt-index="{{ opt_index }}" data-opt-value="{{ value | escape }}" aria-pressed="{% if v.options[opt_index] == value %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}" aria-label="{{ value | escape }}" title="{{ value | escape }}"><img src="{{ value.swatch.image | image_url: width: 96, height: 144, crop: 'center' }}" srcset="{{ value.swatch.image | image_url: width: 96, height: 144, crop: 'center' }} 2x, {{ value.swatch.image | image_url: width: 144, height: 216, crop: 'center' }} 3x" width="48" height="72" alt="" loading="lazy"></button>
            {%- elsif value.swatch.color -%}
              {%- comment -%} data-swatch-set tells theme.js's paintSwatches to
              leave this one alone — without it the table would paint over the
              colour the merchant actually chose. {%- endcomment -%}
              <button type="button" class="swatch" data-swatch="{{ value | escape }}" data-swatch-set style="background-color: {{ value.swatch.color }};" data-opt-btn data-opt-index="{{ opt_index }}" data-opt-value="{{ value | escape }}" aria-pressed="{% if v.options[opt_index] == value %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}" aria-label="{{ value | escape }}" title="{{ value | escape }}"></button>
            {%- elsif cv_media -%}
              <button type="button" class="swatch swatch--img" data-opt-btn data-opt-index="{{ opt_index }}" data-opt-value="{{ value | escape }}" aria-pressed="{% if v.options[opt_index] == value %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}" aria-label="{{ value | escape }}" title="{{ value | escape }}"><img src="{{ cv_media | image_url: width: 96, height: 144, crop: 'center' }}" srcset="{{ cv_media | image_url: width: 96, height: 144, crop: 'center' }} 2x, {{ cv_media | image_url: width: 144, height: 216, crop: 'center' }} 3x" width="48" height="72" alt="" loading="lazy"></button>
            {%- else -%}
              {%- comment -%} Colourless in Liquid on purpose: theme.js paintSwatches()
              fills these from qvSwatchMap, so the fashion-hex table lives in exactly
              one place (the PLP cards and the quick view share it). {%- endcomment -%}
              <button type="button" class="swatch" data-swatch="{{ value | escape }}" data-opt-btn data-opt-index="{{ opt_index }}" data-opt-value="{{ value | escape }}" aria-pressed="{% if v.options[opt_index] == value %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}" aria-label="{{ value | escape }}" title="{{ value | escape }}"></button>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      {%- else -%}
        {%- comment -%} .sizes is the JS contract name and the quick view reuses it;
        .sizes--boxed is the PDP-only opt-in to the 44x44 tile treatment, so
        widening the tiles here can never inflate the inline row inside the
        desktop rich bar or the injected quick-view row. {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="sizes sizes--boxed" role="group" aria-label="{{ opt_label | escape }}">
          {%- for value in option.values -%}
            {%- liquid
              comment
                TWO availability answers, because they have different lifetimes
                and mixing them is how a picker ends up lying:

                anywhere — no available variant carries this value at all. A
                  property of the PRODUCT, so no option click can change it and
                  the attribute can never go stale. This is the one that earns
                  aria-disabled and the unconditional strike-through.
                here — unavailable with every OTHER option pinned where the
                  shopper currently stands. Same test as theme.js
                  partialFor(test).find(available), so the first paint matches
                  what the JS paints a frame later. It IS selection-dependent,
                  and theme.js re-resolves it into the disabled attribute on
                  every render — which is exactly why the stylesheet must gate
                  data-unavailable-here behind html:not(.js). Styled
                  unconditionally it would stay struck through after a colour
                  change that made the size available again.
              endcomment
              assign value_anywhere = false
              assign value_here = false
              for pv in product.variants
                unless pv.available
                  continue
                endunless
                unless pv.options[opt_index] == value
                  continue
                endunless
                assign value_anywhere = true
                assign combo_ok = true
                for other_option in product.options_with_values
                  assign other_index = forloop.index0
                  unless other_index == opt_index
                    unless pv.options[other_index] == v.options[other_index]
                      assign combo_ok = false
                      break
                    endunless
                  endunless
                endfor
                if combo_ok
                  assign value_here = true
                  break
                endif
              endfor
            -%}
            <button
              type="button"
              class="size-btn"
              data-opt-btn
              data-opt-index="{{ opt_index }}"
              data-opt-value="{{ value | escape }}"
              aria-pressed="{% if v.options[opt_index] == value %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
              {%- unless value_anywhere %} aria-disabled="true" data-unavailable title="{{ 'product.form.option_sold_out' | t }}"{% endunless -%}
              {%- if value_anywhere and value_here == false %} data-unavailable-here title="{{ 'product.form.option_unavailable' | t }}"{% endif -%}
            >{{ value | escape }}</button>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
    {%- comment -%} Return anchor: the phone bar's expansion panel MOVES the group
    above into the sheet (never a duplicate picker to keep in sync) and puts it
    back immediately before this span. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if is_size -%}<span data-size-home hidden></span>{%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endunless -%}
